WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.562
Picture ordering a book online.

2
00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:07.686
That evening, your banking app shows the money gone.

3
00:00:07.686 --> 00:00:12.192
A week later, there's no parcel, and the order page still says: Processing.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.754
Behind that page, the shop's program had a few jobs.

5
00:00:15.754 --> 00:00:16.850
Charge your card.

6
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.817
Set a copy of the book aside in the warehouse.

7
00:00:19.817 --> 00:00:21.880
Wait a day, then book a courier.

8
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.169
In code, that's a few lines, run one after another.

9
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.788
It charged the card.

10
00:00:26.788 --> 00:00:29.701
Then its computer restarted for an update.

11
00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:34.852
Everything the program held in memory, including which line it had reached, was gone.

12
00:00:34.852 --> 00:00:37.553
Nothing will ever come back for the other jobs.

13
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.197
So here's the question.

14
00:00:39.197 --> 00:00:43.147
After a crash, how does a program carry on from where it stopped?

15
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:48.463
The tempting fix: when the program starts again, run the order from the top.

16
00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:51.771
Each step worked the first time, so it feels safe.

17
00:00:51.771 --> 00:00:52.631
Let's try it.

18
00:00:52.631 --> 00:00:53.227
Charge...

19
00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:56.139
and the bank charges the card a second time.

20
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:58.348
Two charges, and still no book.

21
00:00:58.348 --> 00:01:01.479
That's a bookshop with a very odd business model.

22
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:09.931
What we want is the same plain lines of code, and a program that remembers what it already did, even when its memory is wiped.

23
00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:12.417
Picture a shop with workers on shifts.

24
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:16.089
Each order has an instruction sheet, and a notebook beside it.

25
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:23.021
The first worker does a step, then writes it in the notebook: ten oh two, charged the card, here's the receipt.

26
00:01:23.021 --> 00:01:25.807
Sets the book aside, and writes that down too.

27
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:29.661
At the change of shift, the next worker has never seen this order.

28
00:01:29.661 --> 00:01:32.959
So they read the sheet from the top, with the notebook open.

29
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:37.541
Every step the notebook shows, they skip, and use the result written there.

30
00:01:37.541 --> 00:01:40.277
They start work at the first step with no entry.

31
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:43.926
In software, this idea is called durable execution.

32
00:01:43.926 --> 00:01:46.520
Durable means it lasts through a crash.

33
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.974
The sheet is your code, called the workflow: the part that decides what happens next.

34
00:01:51.974 --> 00:01:57.331
The steps that touch the outside world, charge, reserve and ship, are called activities.

35
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:01.694
The notebook is the event history, and each line in it is an event.

36
00:02:01.694 --> 00:02:03.788
And the workers are your programs.

37
00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:13.853
Temporal is one well-known engine that works this way: a free program that keeps the history of every workflow, on a server, in a database of its own.

38
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.966
Watch worker-1 run the order.

39
00:02:15.966 --> 00:02:22.527
At charge, Temporal's library, the part of Temporal inside your program, doesn't call the bank itself.

40
00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:25.229
It asks the server to schedule the charge.

41
00:02:25.229 --> 00:02:27.545
The server writes: Charge scheduled.

42
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:32.680
The bank charges the card, and the server writes: Charge done, with the receipt.

43
00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:36.181
Reserve goes the same way: scheduled, then done.

44
00:02:36.181 --> 00:02:38.926
Then the sleep for a day becomes a timer.

45
00:02:38.926 --> 00:02:41.136
The server writes: Timer started.

46
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:45.372
No thread, no path of work in your program, waits a whole day.

47
00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:47.091
Now we kill worker-1.

48
00:02:47.091 --> 00:02:50.754
Everything it held in memory about this order is gone.

49
00:02:50.754 --> 00:02:52.449
The history is untouched.

50
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:59.067
A day later, the timer fires, and the server hands the order to worker-2, which has never seen it.

51
00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:01.156
So who calls the bank this time?

52
00:03:01.156 --> 00:03:02.201
Pause and guess.

53
00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:08.292
Worker-2 runs the code from the first line.

54
00:03:08.292 --> 00:03:09.584
Wait, from the top?

55
00:03:09.584 --> 00:03:11.897
That's the fix that charged twice.

56
00:03:11.897 --> 00:03:13.393
Here's the difference.

57
00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:19.514
At charge, the library finds Charge done in the history, and hands back the receipt at once.

58
00:03:19.514 --> 00:03:20.912
The bank isn't called.

59
00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:24.053
Reserve: its result comes from the history too.

60
00:03:24.053 --> 00:03:27.921
And the sleep returns at once, because its timer already fired.

61
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.072
Then the code reaches ship.

62
00:03:30.072 --> 00:03:31.736
There's no event for it.

63
00:03:31.736 --> 00:03:35.064
So this time, the library schedules it for real.

64
00:03:35.064 --> 00:03:35.757
Ship done.

65
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.119
The order returns: SHIPPED.

66
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.004
The lesson's demo prints the same.

67
00:03:41.004 --> 00:03:45.754
Worker-2 ran charge zero times, reserve zero times, ship once.

68
00:03:45.754 --> 00:03:47.209
Cards charged: one.

69
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:51.079
Running the code again against its history is called replay.

70
00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:54.876
A colleague who reads the notes before touching anything.

71
00:03:54.876 --> 00:03:56.415
Every shop could use one.

72
00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.695
Three things to keep in mind.

73
00:03:58.695 --> 00:04:02.531
One: the workflow code never touches the outside world itself.

74
00:04:02.531 --> 00:04:08.163
It runs again on every replay, so a bank call written straight into it would run again too.

75
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.805
Two: a healthy worker rarely replays.

76
00:04:10.805 --> 00:04:16.003
It keeps recent orders in memory, and the server sends its next task back to it.

77
00:04:16.003 --> 00:04:18.148
Replay is for when a worker dies.

78
00:04:18.433 --> 00:04:20.947
Three: here's where the notebook breaks.

79
00:04:20.947 --> 00:04:25.976
A worker who charged the card, and died before writing it down, leaves no trace.

80
00:04:25.976 --> 00:04:27.861
So the next one charges again.

81
00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:32.321
That's why each step still needs a key, so the bank can spot a repeat.

82
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.488
So how does a program carry on after a crash?

83
00:04:35.488 --> 00:04:36.772
It doesn't remember.

84
00:04:36.772 --> 00:04:39.404
It writes every step down outside itself.

85
00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:49.283
Then any worker can replay the code against those notes: finished steps hand back their recorded answers, and real work starts at the first step with no entry.

86
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:50.854
One quick check.

87
00:04:50.854 --> 00:04:55.897
Suppose worker-1 dies right after Reserve done, before the sleep starts.

88
00:04:55.897 --> 00:04:57.087
Worker-2 replays.

89
00:04:57.087 --> 00:04:59.889
What's the first thing it does for real?

90
00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:01.009
Pause and think.

91
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.482
It starts the timer.

92
00:05:05.482 --> 00:05:11.125
Charge and reserve come from the history, and the sleep is the first step with no entry.
